(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_statesofmatter=[{id:"neet-2023-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Boyle's law graphs",q:"Which amongst the following options is correct graphical representation of Boyle\u2019s Law ?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:2,s:`Boyle's law: PV = nRT, so P = nRT \xD7 (1/V).
At a fixed T, P against 1/V is a straight line through the origin with slope nRT.
A higher temperature gives a steeper line, so T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081 with T\u2083 the steepest.
This is the graph in option 3.`,fig:{w:480,h:335,alt:"Four graphs of Boyle's law options: (1) P against T with straight lines for V1 < V2 < V3; (2) P against V with hyperbolas for T3 > T2 > T1; (3) P against 1/V with straight lines for T3 > T2 > T1; (4) P against 1/V with curves for T3 > T2 > T1.",d:[["l",56,140,56,25,"a"],["l",56,140,196,140,"a"],["t",2,72,"(1)",""],["l",38,58,38,42,"a"],["t",38,74,"P","m"],["t",116,164,"T","m"],["l",134,156,164,156,"a"],["l",56,140,131,35,""],["t",134,34,"V\u2081",""],["l",56,140,158,47,""],["t",161,46,"V\u2082",""],["l",56,140,184,59,""],["t",187,58,"V\u2083",""],["t",102,120,"V\u2081 < V\u2082 < V\u2083",""],["l",296,140,296,25,"a"],["l",296,140,436,140,"a"],["t",242,72,"(2)",""],["l",278,58,278,42,"a"],["t",278,74,"P","m"],["t",356,164,"V","m"],["l",374,156,404,156,"a"],["p","M310 40C311.3 45.9 314.4 65.7 317.7 75.5C321 85.2 324.5 92.1 329.6 98.3C334.7 104.6 340.2 109.1 348.1 113.1C355.9 117.2 364.5 120 376.7 122.6C388.8 125.3 413.6 127.8 421 128.8",""],["t",426,131.8,"T\u2083","s"],["p","M324 40C325.6 44.3 329.9 58.4 333.8 65.9C337.6 73.4 341.8 79.5 346.9 85C352.1 90.6 357.8 95.1 364.7 99.2C371.7 103.4 379.3 106.7 388.7 109.8C398.1 112.8 415.6 116.3 421 117.6",""],["t",426,120.6,"T\u2082","s"],["p","M339 40C340.7 43.2 345.4 53.4 349.2 59.2C353 65 357.2 70.1 361.9 74.7C366.6 79.4 371.7 83.5 377.6 87.3C383.4 91.1 389.7 94.4 397 97.4C404.2 100.5 417 104.2 421 105.6",""],["t",426,108.6,"T\u2081","s"],["t",350,46,"T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081",""],["l",56,310,56,195,"a"],["l",56,310,196,310,"a"],["t",2,242,"(3)",""],["l",38,228,38,212,"a"],["t",38,244,"P","m"],["t",116,334,"1/V","m"],["l",134,326,164,326,"a"],["l",56,310,131,205,""],["t",134,204,"T\u2083",""],["l",56,310,158,217,""],["t",161,216,"T\u2082",""],["l",56,310,184,229,""],["t",187,228,"T\u2081",""],["t",122,290,"T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081",""],["l",296,310,296,195,"a"],["l",296,310,436,310,"a"],["t",242,242,"(4)",""],["l",278,228,278,212,"a"],["t",278,244,"P","m"],["t",356,334,"1/V","m"],["l",374,326,404,326,"a"],["p","M310 210C311.3 215.9 314.4 235.7 317.7 245.5C321 255.2 324.5 262.1 329.6 268.3C334.7 274.6 340.2 279.1 348.1 283.1C355.9 287.2 364.5 290 376.7 292.6C388.8 295.3 413.6 297.8 421 298.8",""],["t",426,301.8,"T\u2081","s"],["p","M324 210C325.6 214.3 329.9 228.4 333.8 235.9C337.6 243.4 341.8 249.5 346.9 255C352.1 260.6 357.8 265.1 364.7 269.2C371.7 273.4 379.3 276.7 388.7 279.8C398.1 282.8 415.6 286.3 421 287.6",""],["t",426,290.6,"T\u2082","s"],["p","M339 210C340.7 213.2 345.4 223.4 349.2 229.2C353 235 357.2 240.1 361.9 244.7C366.6 249.4 371.7 253.5 377.6 257.3C383.4 261.1 389.7 264.4 397 267.4C404.2 270.5 417 274.2 421 275.6",""],["t",426,278.6,"T\u2083","s"],["t",350,216,"T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081",""]]},sfig:{w:300,h:165,alt:"P against 1/V: three straight lines through the origin for T3 > T2 > T1, with T3 the steepest.",d:[["l",110,132,110,10,"a"],["l",110,132,280,132,"a"],["l",66,76,66,58,"a"],["t",64,96,"Pressure (P)","m"],["t",120,156,"Volume (1/V)",""],["l",212,148,246,148,"a"],["l",110,132,190,14,""],["t",193,13,"T\u2083",""],["l",110,132,213,28,""],["t",216,27,"T\u2082",""],["l",110,132,235,40,""],["t",238,39,"T\u2081",""],["t",176,110,"T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081",""]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Intermolecular forces",q:`Intermolecular forces are forces of attraction and repulsion between interacting particles that will include :
A. dipole-dipole forces.
B. dipole-induced dipole forces.
C. hydrogen bonding.
D. covalent bonding.
E. dispersion forces.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, C, D, E are correct.","B, C, D, E are correct.","A, B, C, D are correct.","A, B, C, E are correct."],c:3,s:`Intermolecular forces act between molecules.
Covalent bonds hold atoms together inside a molecule, so D is not included.
Dipole-dipole, dipole-induced dipole, hydrogen bonding and dispersion forces are intermolecular.
So A, B, C and E are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Dalton's law of partial pressures",q:"Which one is not correct mathematical equation for Dalton\u2019s Law of partial pressure? Here p = total pressure of gaseous mixture. (where p\u1D62 = partial pressure of ith gas, \u03C7\u1D62 = mole fraction of ith gas in gaseous mixture, p\xB0\u1D62 = pressure of ith gas in pure state)",o:["p = n\u2081(RT)/(V) + n\u2082(RT)/(V) + n\u2083(RT)/(V)","p\u1D62 = \u03C7\u1D62p","p\u1D62 = \u03C7\u1D62p\xB0\u1D62","p = p\u2081 + p\u2082 + p\u2083"],c:2,s:`Dalton's law: the total pressure of non-reacting gases is the sum of the partial pressures, p = p\u2081 + p\u2082 + p\u2083.
Also p\u1D62 = \u03C7\u1D62p.
Using p = nRT/V for each gas gives p = (n\u2081 + n\u2082 + n\u2083)RT/V.
So options 1, 2 and 4 are correct.
Option 3, p\u1D62 = \u03C7\u1D62p\xB0\u1D62, is not part of Dalton's law (it is Raoult's law form).`},{id:"neet-2022r-q085",pn:85,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Liquefaction and critical temperature",q:`Four gas cylinders containing He, N\u2082, CO\u2082 and NH\u2083 gases separately are gradually cooled from a temperature of 500 K. Which gas will liquify first ?
(Given Tc in K - He : 5.3, N\u2082 : 126, CO\u2082 : 304.1 and NH\u2083 : 405.5)`,o:["NH\u2083","He","N\u2082","CO\u2082"],c:0,s:`A gas can be liquefied only below its critical temperature.
So the gas with the highest critical temperature liquefies first on cooling.
NH\u2083 has the highest Tc (405.5 K), so it liquefies first.`},{id:"neet-2022-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Ideal gas equation",q:"A 10.0 L flask contains 64 g of oxygen at 27\xBAC. (Assume O\u2082 gas is behaving ideally). The pressure inside the flask in bar is (Given R = 0.0831 L bar K\u207B\xB9 mol\u207B\xB9)",o:["498.6","49.8","4.9","2.5"],c:2,s:`PV = nRT, so P = nRT/V.
n = 64/32 = 2 mol, T = 27 + 273 = 300 K.
P = (2 \xD7 0.0831 \xD7 300)/(10) \u2248 4.9 bar.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q091",pn:91,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Boyle's law",q:`A vessel contains 3.2 g of dioxygen gas at STP (273.15 K and 1 atm pressure). The gas is now transferred to another vessel at constant temperature, where pressure becomes one third of the original pressure. The volume of new vessel in L is:
(Given - molar volume at STP is 22.4 L)`,o:["67.2","6.72","2.24","22.4"],c:1,s:`Moles of O\u2082 = 3.2/32 = 0.1 mol.
Volume at STP = 0.1 \xD7 22.4 = 2.24 L.
Boyle's law: P\u2081V\u2081 = P\u2082V\u2082.
1 \xD7 2.24 = (1/3) \xD7 V\u2082.
V\u2082 = 6.72 L.`}]})();
